WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
We've all tapped an app icon, stared at a blank screen,

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.960
and felt that agonizing second where the entire system

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.320
just stalls out.

4
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.160
For years, if that application was built with React Native on Android,

5
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.880
the culprit behind that delay was almost always

6
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.240
the legacy JavaScript core engine, or JSC.

7
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.200
JSC was originally built for web browsers.

8
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.120
When dropped into a mobile environment,

9
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.320
it forced the devices CPU to parse and compile

10
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.280
megabytes of raw JavaScript text at runtime,

11
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.480
completely blocking the main thread before a single pixel could

12
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.320
render.

13
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.840
Meta engineered a way around this structural flaw

14
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.320
by building Hermes.

15
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.160
Rather than trying to parse text faster at launch,

16
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.400
Hermes alters the timeline of when compilation actually

17
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.360
happens.

18
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.520
This chart compares the time to first frame

19
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.600
on a standard mid-range device.

20
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.160
The legacy JSC engine took around 1,400 milliseconds

21
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.000
to render.

22
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.320
Hermes cuts that down to roughly 800 milliseconds.

23
00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.000
Reclaiming over half a second on a cold start

24
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.920
is the precise margin that elevates

25
00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.120
an app from feeling like a sluggish web wrapper

26
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.360
to a truly native experience.

27
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.440
To achieve that speed, Hermes relies

28
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.360
on a head-of-time compilation.

29
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.800
If you write native Android apps,

30
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.520
you already use this exact optimization.

31
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.200
In the standard native pipeline,

32
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.960
Kotlin files are transformed during the build phase,

33
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.640
first into .class, and finally into a pre-compiled .dex

34
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.800
executable.

35
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.640
Hermes intentionally mirrors this architecture.

36
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.320
Instead of shipping raw text, the metrobundler

37
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.720
works with the Hermes compiler to convert your source

38
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.680
files directly into Hermes bytecode, resulting

39
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.600
in a .hbc file.

40
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.080
This compiled bytecode is then packaged directly

41
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.720
into the final Android APK, completely

42
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.600
replacing the traditional raw JavaScript bundle.

43
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.440
Because it strips out human readable text and unoptimized

44
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.960
logic, this resulting bytecode is roughly 20% to 30%

45
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.600
smaller than the equivalent minified JavaScript.

46
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.720
When the user eventually opens the app,

47
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.440
the device skips the text parsing phase entirely

48
00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.640
and executes the pre-compiled bytecode immediately.

49
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.520
By treating JavaScript exactly like native Kotlin code

50
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.600
during the build phase, Hermes permanently

51
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.560
shifts the heaviest computational burden off the user's phone

52
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.440
and onto your build server.

53
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.360
In this launch timeline comparison,

54
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.200
the legacy engine is completely blocked

55
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.080
by the parse and compile phase.

56
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.560
With a head-of-time compilation,

57
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.080
that parse time drops instantaneously to 0 milliseconds.

58
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.960
Securing this instant start required a massive architectural

59
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.680
sacrifice, completely removing the just-in-time,

60
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.360
or JIT compiler.

61
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.440
JIT compilers analyze and optimize code at runtime.

62
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.000
This causes sudden CPU spikes, drains the battery,

63
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.480
and creates unpredictable performance hitches.

64
00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.160
By contrast, the Hermes timeline remains perfectly smooth

65
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.200
without warm-up.

66
00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.120
By removing it, Hermes operates strictly

67
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.560
as a pure interpreter of the bytecode it receives.

68
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.040
A pure interpreter will inevitably lose

69
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.240
to a V8, or JSC engine, in absolute peak CPU throughput

70
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.680
when running long, math-heavy scripts.

71
00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.400
But mobile applications rarely

72
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.160
perform sustained, heavy data processing on the main thread.

73
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.800
A mobile interface prioritizes fast, fluid interactivity

74
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.640
and rapid responsiveness.

75
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.400
Sacrificing theoretical peak execution speed

76
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.160
to guarantee instant UI rendering and eliminate thermal spikes

77
00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.160
is the correct mobile first engineering decision.

78
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.240
After addressing the CPU bottlenecks,

79
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.920
the engine had to conform to the second strict constraint

80
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.560
of all mobile operating systems, heavily limited RAM.

81
00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.560
The Hermes generational garbage collector

82
00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.640
divides the memory heap into two distinct zones.

83
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.880
The young generation zone holds short-lived allocations,

84
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.480
storing intermediate values and rapid react state updates.

85
00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.520
Discarded rapidly, the engine runs high-frequency collection

86
00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.720
cycles here, with low pause times.

87
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.640
Surviving objects and persistent state modules

88
00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.560
seamlessly migrate to the old generation zone,

89
00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.600
collected far less frequently.

90
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.240
There is a critical constraint to this architecture.

91
00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.880
All Hermes garbage collection runs entirely

92
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.680
on the main JavaScript thread,

93
00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.600
without any concurrent background execution.

94
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.240
Lacking concurrency means these collection cycles

95
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.560
are strictly stopped the world events.

96
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.080
Whenever the collector runs, script execution

97
00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.640
halts completely.

98
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.480
But by tuning the generational model so tightly,

99
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.600
the engine keeps these pauses under five milliseconds,

100
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.720
preserving a stable 60 frames per second.

101
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.600
Looking at the memory footprint gauge,

102
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.960
this precise execution model successfully

103
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:22.960
halves the overall JavaScript heap size at idle,

104
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.920
dropping it from 35 megabytes on JSC, down to just 18 megabytes.

105
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.000
Managing memory synchronously, but with extreme generational

106
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.160
efficiency, prevents background threads

107
00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.880
from draining the battery, while keeping

108
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.720
the app remarkably lightweight.

109
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.960
Building a highly optimized engine

110
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.640
requires aggressively cutting legacy features

111
00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.920
that don't fit the new constraints.

112
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.680
Highly dynamic JavaScript functions inherently

113
00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.640
break the strict assumptions required

114
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.320
for ahead of time compilation,

115
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.000
so they are intentionally stripped from Hermes.

116
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.680
Features like the eVal function and width statement

117
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.440
are primary casualties.

118
00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.760
They are disabled by default to protect both mobile performance

119
00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.760
and application security.

120
00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.360
This sets a strict rule for the ecosystem.

121
00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.240
If a third-party library relies on executing strings

122
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.160
as code, it is incompatible with mobile constraints

123
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.800
and must be patched.

124
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.120
On the developer experience side, newer react native versions

125
00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.440
using Hermes directly expose a Chrome Dev Tools protocol

126
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.200
or CDP endpoint.

127
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.120
This direct integration unlocks deep profiling capabilities.

128
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.160
You can inspect asynchronous call stacks across boundaries

129
00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.320
and capture exact memory heap snapshots

130
00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.360
right from the physical device.

131
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.320
While it enforces stricter, modern coding standards,

132
00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.720
Hermes repays the developer by providing desktop grade

133
00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.680
analytical tools running directly on the mobile hardware.

134
00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.800
You can capture a raw sampling profiler trace

135
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.200
straight from a development build using tools

136
00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.840
like flippers Hermes debugger plugin.

137
00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.160
That trace file can then be loaded natively

138
00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.120
into a browser to audit your real world performance data.

139
00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.520
Observing this trace confirms the architectural claims.

140
00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.040
Developers see a predictable flat execution curve

141
00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.560
entirely free from the chaotic interruptions

142
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.480
of runtime compilation.

143
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.400
Empirical trace data proves the theoretical benefits

144
00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.680
of the interpreter model, giving developers

145
00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.760
complete visibility into their thread performance.

146
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.280
When observing a typical react native app

147
00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.560
running on mid-range Android hardware,

148
00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.120
the holistic impact of Hermes is clear.

149
00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.240
This dashboard locks in the final aggregated benchmarks.

150
00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.520
The total elimination of parse time

151
00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.480
roughly 800 milliseconds to first frame,

152
00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.440
a minimized heap size, all for a minimal two megabyte cost

153
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.720
to the final APK size.

154
00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.720
Because these performance gains are so foundational,

155
00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.360
utilizing Hermes is now strictly mandatory

156
00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:50.120
for all apps running the react native new architecture.

157
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.200
But this leaves us with a critical structural question.

158
00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.120
If the processing engine is finally fast enough,

159
00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.120
how do we fix the crumbling narrow span connecting it

160
00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:00.520
to the native side?

161
00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.240
That remaining bottleneck is the legacy asynchronous bridge,

162
00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:07.520
an outdated serialized queue that historically dictated

163
00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.480
how JavaScript and native code communicated.

164
00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.120
Replacing this bridge requires the implementation

165
00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.760
of the JavaScript interface, or JSI,

166
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.040
to finally achieve synchronous zero copy communication

167
00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.280
across the entire framework.

